My name is Eric Nance and I am the host of this series and thank you so much for joining

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:10.520
me.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:16.120
We are kicking off a multi-part series with RStudio software engineer and member of the

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.879
Shiny team, Barret Schloerke.

00:00:18.879 --> 00:00:23.400
We talked about so many great ideas that it couldn't fit into just one episode.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.520
We lead off this series with learning more about Barret's background and his journey

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:34.240
to the RStudio team, as well as learning about the excellent React Logpack framework

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.040
with some great demonstrations.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.740
Let's not wait any longer.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.680
Let's connect with part one of our series with Barret.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.680
That's right.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:44.680
That's right.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:45.680
Yep.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:46.680
Well, welcome everybody.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.320
We have another exciting episode of the Shiny Developer Series on tap.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:56.400
And I am pleased to be joined by RStudio's software engineer, Barret Schloerke.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.840
Barret, thank you so much for joining me today.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.840
Ah, thank you for having me, Eric.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.119
I really appreciate it.

00:01:02.119 --> 00:01:05.400
Yeah, we're really excited to talk about some really cool topics.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.559
So I thought first, why don't you give us a bit about your background and how you discovered

00:01:09.559 --> 00:01:12.120
R and then by proxy, the Shiny package.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:19.639
Yeah, so my background, I am a computer engineer undergrad and I got that at Iowa State.

00:01:19.639 --> 00:01:24.800
And that is actually where I discovered R. I was in a freshman honors seminar and I just

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:31.040
happened to be in a class taught by Hadley Wickham and Heike Hoffman about data visualization.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:36.760
And each week they offered up a new visualization tool to look at data.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.720
And we just, you know, talked about that.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:44.199
And at the very end of the seminar, he goes, hey, if anyone like a research position doing

00:01:44.199 --> 00:01:46.959
stuff like this, let me know if you're interested.

00:01:46.959 --> 00:01:50.400
And I was like, I can get paid to do this?

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.400
This is great.

00:01:51.599 --> 00:01:55.519
That's a nice way to get involved, right?

00:01:55.519 --> 00:01:57.760
So I contacted him and said, this sounds great.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.080
I want to do this.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:07.800
And I joined up with the research group with Dai Cook, Hadley, Heike Hoffman, and Michael

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.279
Lawrence, who were all there at Iowa State during that time.

00:02:11.279 --> 00:02:13.360
And it was a lot of fun.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:20.199
And I just did that as my side research while doing my undergrad there at Iowa State.

00:02:20.199 --> 00:02:22.720
To me, it was software engineering.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.000
But to them, it was statistics.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.800
And it, you know, just the application is towards statistics.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.880
And I think that is really useful in having that context of statistics.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:39.399
But in, you know, deep down for me, it was just coding at the heart of it.

00:02:39.399 --> 00:02:41.559
But anyways, I finished up at Iowa State.

00:02:41.559 --> 00:02:48.199
And then I did a quick pit stop out in San Francisco for a year and a half.

00:02:48.199 --> 00:02:52.360
And I was a front-end engineer for a company called Metamarkets.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.399
They've since been dissolved.

00:02:54.399 --> 00:02:57.639
But I was writing mostly in JavaScript.

00:02:57.639 --> 00:03:03.559
And I was working on a project called DVL, which is Data Visualization Legos, which wrapped

00:03:03.559 --> 00:03:10.880
around D3 and just made it a little bit smaller interface rather than the very complex and

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.240
complicated D3 interface.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.240
And it was nice.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.240
It was really good.

00:03:16.279 --> 00:03:19.679
But that kind of gave me that foundation of reactivity.

00:03:19.679 --> 00:03:23.240
And if I change this input, then I'll change all these outputs.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.039
And so that gave me a pretty strong foundation.

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:30.399
So after San Francisco, I continued on into grad school.

00:03:30.399 --> 00:03:36.160
I did a PhD in statistics at Purdue University.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.919
I was under the direction of Dr. Ryan Hafen and Dr. William Cleveland.

00:03:41.199 --> 00:03:50.479
Ryan is more known for his bokeh work in R. But I personally am very excited about his

00:03:50.479 --> 00:03:52.639
Trelliscope and Trelliscope.js work.

00:03:52.639 --> 00:03:53.639
Yes.

00:03:53.639 --> 00:03:54.639
Me too.

00:03:54.639 --> 00:03:57.080
I'm actually using that for a very important app as I speak.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.919
And my VP that I showed it to absolutely loves it.

00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:02.679
So I'm so thankful that Ryan made that.

00:04:02.679 --> 00:04:03.679
Yes.

00:04:03.679 --> 00:04:07.820
And that was actually, we could say, one of my first introductions to Shiny.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:11.000
Because Trelliscope was written in Shiny.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.860
And we had done the proof of concept and we weren't approaching things properly.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:21.279
And we said we need to spend the next 500 hours or next 100 hours optimizing this to

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:24.720
try to turn it into an HTML widget.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.519
And that way, we can then have multiple Trelliscopes and it's a lot faster.

00:04:28.519 --> 00:04:29.519
Right.

00:04:29.519 --> 00:04:33.779
And you can port it into just a regular document.

00:04:33.779 --> 00:04:37.720
But one of the things that came out of that while I was there is I built a package called

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:44.700
Autocog or I think, yeah, Autocogs and it created automatic cognostics, which are used

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.320
inside Trelliscope.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.980
So if you have a ggplot object, there's no reason I can't inspect that object and say,

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:54.019
oh, you're doing a linear model with the scatterplot.

00:04:54.019 --> 00:04:57.859
Let me give you the range of your X and Y values, your slope and intercept for your

00:04:57.859 --> 00:05:01.239
linear model and your R squared value.

00:05:01.239 --> 00:05:06.079
And then that's automatically added to your Trelliscope so that you can sort and filter

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.500
and do all of that.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.140
So if you're using it for your VP, check it out.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:19.299
I know my wife is I know my wife is using it for sorting different traffic data that

00:05:19.299 --> 00:05:22.260
she's using. So, yeah, it just makes my so much easier.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:24.380
I don't have to build all this up from scratch.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:29.019
It's just let them do the organizing they want and then, yeah, drill down if they want.

00:05:29.019 --> 00:05:31.339
It's very cool. So excellent.

00:05:31.339 --> 00:05:33.739
Yeah. And so those are some awesome people to learn from.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.600
I'm a bit jealous, you know, that's a lot of great knowledge in that group.

00:05:39.359 --> 00:05:44.000
Yes. Thank you. It's also, as you know, happened to be at the right place at the right

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.839
time. If I would have been at a different school or a different year, you know, I would

00:05:47.839 --> 00:05:50.320
have missed that seminar and who knows where I'd be.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.760
So, yep, yep.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.200
Life always works in a serious way sometimes.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.480
And then, yeah, maybe tell us a bit about how you found your way to the RStudio team.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:07.420
So since I had had tasted, you know, work life and I wasn't in school anymore, like I

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.420
was really going to go back to school and finish that up.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:15.380
And but with the intent that I was going back to industry.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:20.500
And so I try to always position myself that even though the work that I'm doing is

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:27.420
statistics for the PhD, it's going to have application towards industry and towards, you

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.320
know, like this is a chance for me to build a two or three year resume that I can use to

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:37.160
like say, hey, I am your candidate to work on on the RStudio team.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:41.720
And so I think it worked out, worked out well.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:48.480
So, yeah, I had a conversation with Hadley and, you know, because we're good friends at

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:54.600
that point and then got a got a conversation with Tarif and then had to earn my way

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.200
through through Joe and the team.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.140
So excellent.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.179
Yeah. You've been a wonderful addition.

00:07:00.179 --> 00:07:03.660
And this actually is a nice lead into when I first met you.

00:07:04.059 --> 00:07:09.700
It probably wasn't that long after you joined, but you and I spoke at the same RStudio

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:14.660
comp session. It was a shiny session where I was talking about the effective use of shiny

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:19.220
modules because I've been a big proponent of modules and you came right afterwards.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:22.339
And you probably saw my face in the front row.

00:07:22.339 --> 00:07:26.480
I was kind of decompressing from my talk because that's the first time I spoke in front

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.160
of so many people. But then you gave a great talk about some excellent work you've done

00:07:31.399 --> 00:07:34.279
working on a package called React Log.

00:07:34.279 --> 00:07:38.640
And so I want to start with maybe showcasing some of the work you've done with React

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:44.480
Log and why it's such an important tool for shiny users to leverage to understand their

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.119
reactivity and their shiny apps.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:47.880
Maybe we could go to the demo now.

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:55.299
Absolutely. So React Log is a way for users to look inside the black box of shiny, and I

00:07:55.299 --> 00:08:03.000
think it is so great because I've seen many different like napkin drawings by by shiny

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.739
developers where they're like, I believe this input connects the output and it does it

00:08:07.739 --> 00:08:09.959
this way. Here's my napkin.

00:08:10.859 --> 00:08:12.220
Guilty as charged on that one.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:17.799
Yeah. And so I knew there's got to be a way that we can record this.

00:08:17.799 --> 00:08:21.000
And Joe had already implemented something beforehand.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:26.160
But it, you know, after about your toy example, it didn't really scale that well.

00:08:26.619 --> 00:08:30.640
And we needed something that was a full fledged network graphic and you could search, you

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:32.440
could go backwards and forwards easily.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.200
And so I just was given a clean slate and said, hey, implement this.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:45.340
So for React Log, what it will do is that we can look at this and this is just a replaying

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:48.820
GIF, but we can imagine an example like.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:56.900
Pythagoras theorem, right, we have our input A and our input B, and as I change the values

00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:01.440
of A, in this case, it's calculating, it will calculate A squared and B squared, we can

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:06.099
then calculate C squared and then, you know, we take square root to C and then we can

00:09:06.099 --> 00:09:10.320
display it in our output as text or verbatim, just so we have some multiple outputs.

00:09:11.159 --> 00:09:17.880
And you can see here, if it's green, then the value is ready and ready to be used.

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:22.200
If it's dark gray, it's invalidating, like it's trying to get rid of all the people that

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.559
depend on it. If it's light gray, it hasn't been calculated yet, but exists.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:31.239
And if it's orange or this orange is yellow color, it's calculating its value.

00:09:32.559 --> 00:09:35.000
So I will do a quick demo here.

00:09:35.979 --> 00:09:43.260
So I have that app up here in the RStudio IDE, and I'm going to go ahead and run the

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:49.460
app. I'm going to do it in the browser just because the key bindings are just a little

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:59.979
funny. And so I can change the value to 5 and 12 and we get, you know, the 5, 12, 13

00:09:59.979 --> 00:10:01.159
triangle. Great.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:09.000
And if I open up React log with command FN3 or F3, then I get the React log right away.

00:10:10.679 --> 00:10:14.119
This shows up at the first time that Shiny is idle.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:18.000
And in this case, all of our outputs and inputs have been calculated.

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:23.880
So, you know, this app isn't too exciting because everything is working as expected, but

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:31.500
you can see when I change the value to 5, then now all the downstream dependencies will

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:32.820
start to invalidate.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:36.260
B and B squared don't invalidate because they weren't changed.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:41.659
And then once everything's invalidated, we then calculate our outputs like the output C

00:10:41.659 --> 00:10:42.820
text and verbatim.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:47.859
And so they calculate upstream, retrieving the values as necessary and only calculating

00:10:47.859 --> 00:10:53.179
what's necessary. And then when we change B, the same pattern happened as well.

00:10:54.739 --> 00:11:02.500
The part, though, where it becomes really useful is when you believe something should work and it

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:08.039
doesn't. And you can React log can't really tell you that your app is wrong, but it can tell you

00:11:08.039 --> 00:11:08.900
what's going on.

00:11:09.219 --> 00:11:11.900
Yeah, the first step is always knowing where the problem is.

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:12.940
Where?

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:14.099
It's very hard to find.

00:11:14.979 --> 00:11:18.619
Yeah. So we have our mental model of what things should be done.

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:22.419
OK, so this one is where the Pythagoras theorem is broken.

00:11:22.479 --> 00:11:25.039
We have three, four and a five triangle.

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:27.880
The C value here is not five.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:29.200
So that's not expected.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:37.119
Right. And if we change A to 5 and B to 12, we should have the 13 as our answer.

00:11:37.119 --> 00:11:38.960
And C is still wrong.

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:40.799
And this one is not 13.

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:42.919
So that's not good.

00:11:43.559 --> 00:11:47.359
So let's open up a React log, the command FN3 on Mac.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:50.799
And we see a different shape than what we had last time.

00:11:50.799 --> 00:11:53.820
Last time it was two inputs and two outputs.

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:59.659
Now it's kind of a cone and this one isn't even connected to anything.

00:12:01.099 --> 00:12:06.500
So there's a reason why that C verbatim is not being correct.

00:12:06.739 --> 00:12:11.099
And then we can also see that C squared pulls in A squared, B squared.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:15.979
Good. But B squared pulls in A that does not pull in input B.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:22.280
So we could come to our code and validate that and say, ah, B squared does pull in A.

00:12:22.359 --> 00:12:23.239
That's a mistake.

00:12:24.359 --> 00:12:28.640
And C squared here is isolating on B squared.

00:12:28.919 --> 00:12:32.719
We didn't see that, but this answer is just five.

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:37.239
So we should probably switch that to answer and then we can move on.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:44.000
So I think you can you can kind of see where, yeah, in this example, you're able to fix them

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.380
fairly quickly, but you might have in a production setting, React is a whole bunch of

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:52.020
inputs and outputs connected.

00:12:52.059 --> 00:12:57.179
And this having this visual just helps cognitively figure out where are the

00:12:57.179 --> 00:12:59.900
disconnects where we're just looking in code.

00:13:00.299 --> 00:13:05.260
It's it's hard enough to match up your input names of the right reactive references and all

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:10.099
that. So this is certainly something that had this been around in this form when I was

00:13:10.119 --> 00:13:14.200
first learning Shiny, would have helped me understand reactivity a lot better.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:20.320
Yeah. Having that visual tool of knowing that I change A and this is how it changes

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.080
downstream is just invaluable.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:24.520
It's not just magic anymore.

00:13:24.719 --> 00:13:26.280
There's a thing to explain it.

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:34.520
So talking about the larger applications, this is actually the React log for Joe Chang's

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:36.719
Cranwhales application that he built.

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:42.460
Yes. And so I think we can probably just kind of link to that at some point.

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:44.340
But we'll have that in the show notes for this.

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:51.739
Yep. And but what it does, it has like a plot and it also has some numbers and it tries to

00:13:51.739 --> 00:13:56.979
tell you who is the major offenders for downloading all of the CRAN packages and causing

00:13:56.979 --> 00:13:58.099
all the bandwidth issues.

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:01.859
And this is where we start to get a little bit more complicated.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:05.159
It's a lot taller than it was last time.

00:14:05.479 --> 00:14:10.520
And it has, you know, one, two, three, four, five, six, seven dependency layers.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:13.760
So it's quite a complicated application.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:20.159
But one of the things that I like is that we have our plot object that we're putting into

00:14:20.159 --> 00:14:21.719
the downloads per hour.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:28.559
And if I double click on it, I can actually search into its family tree, both, you know,

00:14:28.739 --> 00:14:30.599
ancestors and descendants.

00:14:31.059 --> 00:14:37.059
So if I even calculate maybe our whale downloads, I can see all the things that are

00:14:37.059 --> 00:14:38.619
involved with that whale downloads.

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:45.859
This is really useful when you have many different reactive objects.

00:14:46.859 --> 00:14:51.219
I've seen some apps have like 2000 reactive IDs on load.

00:14:51.739 --> 00:14:55.340
Wow. And yeah, it is.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:01.580
And so when that happens, if hopefully everything has been labeled properly, but I like

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:03.280
to use the search bar in the top right.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:09.919
So I'll say like whale and now two or three things have been been hit by that.

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:13.880
If I do whale underscore now, it's just that whale underscore downloads.

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:16.280
Oh, that's amazing to save time.

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:20.159
And so I'm just kind of like poking and hoping for the best that you find the right

00:15:20.159 --> 00:15:23.440
areas. Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:23.619 --> 00:15:29.020
And when I move forward in the timeline, I'm using the arrow keys on my computer or I

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:31.900
can use the buttons up top, depending on how far you want to skip.

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:39.859
The jumps will only be for this particular subset of elements.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:47.299
So if you see the number up top, it goes from 201 directly to 207 because maybe 202

00:15:47.299 --> 00:15:49.619
and three were some things that weren't involved.

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:55.320
OK, but it's nice because then you only are looking at your subset and you can see

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.880
what's going on. Right.

00:15:58.359 --> 00:16:02.400
I guess this begs the question, obviously, yeah, the whales app is one of the bigger

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:03.719
ones you could use this with.

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:08.320
I mean, I know you've been building this with scalability in mind, but have you

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:11.000
encountered apps that are almost too big for this yet?

00:16:11.039 --> 00:16:13.719
Or is it we're kind of in the cases that you've tried this with?

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:21.059
I have encountered a few apps and it's very encouraging to see that I think it's

00:16:21.059 --> 00:16:26.219
really neat. It's too big when it is not filtered.

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:33.859
When you filter down just a little bit, you know, if I do this animation here and

00:16:33.859 --> 00:16:39.739
there's 2000 edges that you're moving around, that's a lot of computation time.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:47.000
And so maybe I've thought about opening up settings to say, you know, for this

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:51.280
visualization tool that we're using, the library, maybe we add in the option of like

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:57.000
do not animate because then it would just jump jittery for a user experience.

